# Migration: EventProcessor to .NET 8

## Key Problems with the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 8 — it was removed entirely.
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()` is obsolete** and unreliable — it only works when the thread is in a managed wait state.
3. **Race condition**: Multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose items (only one `Dequeue` per signal).
4. **No graceful shutdown** mechanism — the loop runs `while(true)` with no cancellation.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _processingTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel is closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();           // signal no more items
        _cts?.Cancel();                       // cancel if items are still draining

        if (_processingTask is not null)
        {
            using var timeoutCts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
            try
            {
                await _processingTask.WaitAsync(timeoutCts.Token);
            }
            catch (TimeoutException)
            {
                // Task didn't finish in time — it will observe cancellation eventually
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // Your processing logic here
    }
}
```

## If You Need a Synchronous API (closer to original shape)

```csharp
using System.Collections.Concurrent;

public class EventProcessorSync : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop) { IsBackground = true };
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
                Handle(item);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _queue.CompleteAdding();               // signals end-of-stream
        if (!_thread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)) ?? true)
            _cts.Cancel();                    // force cancellation — no Abort needed
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _queue.Dispose();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls Summary

| .NET Framework Pattern | .NET 8 Replacement | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` | `Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `CancellationToken` | Obsolete, marked `[Obsolete]` in .NET 8 |
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` | `Channel<T>` or `BlockingCollection<T>` | Eliminates race conditions, built-in backpressure |
| `while(true)` spin | `ReadAllAsync(ct)` / `GetConsumingEnumerable(ct)` | Cooperative cancellation |
| `Thread.Join` + `Abort` fallback | `Task.WaitAsync` + timeout | Structured async shutdown |

## Recommendation

Prefer the **`Channel<T>`** version — it's allocation-efficient, async-native, and the idiomatic .NET 8 producer/consumer pattern. Use `BlockingCollection<T>` only if you must stay fully synchronous.
